# local secrets - never commit
local.properties
*.keystore
*.jks
.env
.env.local
secrets.json

# FR-1108's release signing. `keystore.properties` names the keystore's path and holds its
# passwords; `keystore.properties.example` beside it is committed and shows the four keys with
# no values. A signing config that read from a committed file would put the passwords for the
# upload key in the repository, which is the whole of what this line prevents.
keystore.properties

# FR-001's Desktop OAuth client, on the same convention and for the same reason.
# `client.properties` holds the client id and secret; `desktop/client.properties.example`
# beside it is committed and shows the two keys with no values.
#
# Google's "secret" for a Desktop client is not confidential in any real sense — it ships
# inside every copy of the application, which is exactly why RFC 8252 requires PKCE for this
# client type and why PKCE rather than the secret protects the exchange. It is kept out anyway:
# a credential in a git history cannot be removed from it, and the id identifies the project's
# Cloud client to anyone who reads the repository.
client.properties

# OS
Thumbs.db
.DS_Store

